Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Abstract", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"90ae79f4-0b20-4f14-b931-3f120c325d14"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This notebook solves the seven exercises at the end of S Shape Functions \
Theory, called the main notebook below. Each problem is stated, solved with a \
derivation, checked numerically and illustrated with plots. Problem 1 repeats \
the staircase experiment with the arctan and algebraic sigmoids. Their tails \
approach the limit 1 like 1/u and 1/",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["u", "2"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "62a45970-ec34-449e-9f3e-be90b9640dc7"],
 " instead of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["e", 
    RowBox[{"-", "u"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5ffdd595-f14e-4872-a17d-26720724a13d"],
 ", so every step leaks into its neighbours; to bring the leakage at N = 20 \
down to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", 
    RowBox[{"-", "3"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8b0bdd1f-a699-485f-aa99-3f8014b008dd"],
 " takes \[Lambda] \[TildeTilde] 235 for the logistic, 401 for the algebraic \
and 6279 for the arctan sigmoid, and a tail formula predicts these values. \
Problem 2 shows that \[Sigma](t) + sin(t) exp(-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["t", "2"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3be46a34-bad4-498f-a5fb-b6280d824c64"],
 ") is sigmoidal but not monotone, overshooting to 1.068, and that its \
staircase still converges like 1/N, as Cybenko's theorem allows. Problem 3 \
uses the activations ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["t", "2"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "47afd86d-1a1d-463d-b9b8-a5d1cc06e598"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["t", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3b27391d-5322-4c69-96c0-82db3dfbd3c8"],
 ": the network can then produce only polynomials of degree 2 or 3, its \
hidden matrix has rank 6 or 10, and the error stops at that of the best \
polynomial (RMS 0.5 and 0.331), which is what the theorem of Leshno, Lin, \
Pinkus and Schocken predicts. Problem 4 writes x y z = exp(ln x + ln y + ln \
z) in the form of Kolmogorov's superposition formula and adds a four-cube \
identity valid for all real inputs. Problem 5 shows that one step of \
BasicRecurrentLayer is a Cybenko sum of the input (x, h), checks this against \
the real layer, and identifies the arrows that make it recurrent. Problem 6 \
derives the gradient with respect to the weights by the path rule, checks it \
against automatic differentiation and finite differences, and uses it to \
train an 8-unit network to a maximum error of 8 \[Times] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", 
    RowBox[{"-", "4"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "92a6787f-be2a-48ca-90c7-e0b1879180a3"],
 ", 34 times smaller than an 80-unit staircase. Problem 7 predicts the \
staircase rates ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["N", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"-", "1"}], "/", "2"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d8418330-4765-4557-aff4-63a9fc2739e4"],
 " for x sin(1/x) and N to the power -ln 2/ln 3 = -0.631 for the Cantor \
staircase from their moduli of continuity, before measuring them, and the \
measurements confirm both. All results are computed in Wolfram Language, and \
every figure is reproducible from the numbered code cells."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fcfa0d60-80b2-4de2-a0a2-86ba80e0b20f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Keywords: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "sigmoidal function; staircase approximation; tail decay; non-monotone \
activation; polynomial activation; \
Leshno\[Dash]Lin\[Dash]Pinkus\[Dash]Schocken theorem; Kolmogorov\[Dash]Arnold \
superposition; BasicRecurrentLayer; backpropagation; gradient descent; \
modulus of continuity; Cantor staircase; Wolfram Language"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"995d6384-6ea6-454b-9a12-ba34d3d5e311"],

Cell["\<\
References of the form Eq. (M7), Figure M3, Table M3, Theorem M8 or cell c.13 \
of the main notebook point to S Shape Functions Theory. Equations, figures \
and tables of this notebook are numbered by problem: Eq. (3.2) is the second \
equation of Problem 3.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2e036388-7731-42b9-ad7a-afbc0801bca6"],

Cell["\<\
Every code cell starts with a comment such as (*c.3.4*) giving its index: \
problem 3, cell 4. Run c.0 and c.0.1 first; after that each problem runs on \
its own, in order. The notebook keeps its variables in a private context \
(CellContext -> Notebook), so definitions from other notebooks in the same \
session cannot interfere with it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7fa46333-58b8-4772-8ab8-64a82a574607"],

