Cell["\<\
Cybenko's Theorem M2 needs only continuity and the two limits of Eq. (M1). \
Monotonicity is never used: Lemma M1 needs only that s(\[Lambda]t) tends to \
the step as \[Lambda] \[RightArrow] \[Infinity], for every t \[NotEqual] 0, \
with s bounded. Figure 2.2 shows exactly that. The overshoot keeps its \
height, 0.068, but is pushed into a window of width about 1/\[Lambda] around \
0. By contrast Theorem M6, of Hornik, Stinchcombe and White, assumes a \
squashing function, which is nondecreasing, and so does not apply to s. \
Theorems M7 and M8 do apply, since s is bounded, continuous and not a \
polynomial.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"142daead-3550-4195-8e14-6a8450cf7093"],

Cell["\<\
Figure 2.2. s(\[Lambda]t) for \[Lambda] = 1, 4, 16 against the step (dashed).\
\
\>", "Caption",
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",
 FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"88c6ab58-9c3b-4584-bd79-790333c28d28"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
(*c.2.3*)
(* steepening: s2(\[Lambda]t) tends to the step except at t = 0; the
   overshoot keeps its height and moves into t = 0 *)
(* numeric copy of s that avoids underflow of exp(-t^2) for large t *)
sNum[t_?NumericQ] := LogisticSigmoid[t] + Sin[t] Exp[-Min[t^2, 700]];
Plot[{sNum[t], sNum[4 t], sNum[16 t], UnitStep[t]},
 {t, -2, 2}, PlotRange -> {-0.15, 1.15}, Exclusions -> None,
 PlotStyle -> {Automatic, Automatic, Automatic,
   Directive[Black, Dashed]}, GridLines -> {None, {0, 1}},
 PlotLegends -> Placed[LineLegend[{\"\[Lambda] = 1\", \"\[Lambda] = 4\", \"\
\[Lambda] = 16\", \"step\"},
    LabelStyle -> figStyle, LegendLayout -> \"Row\"], Below],
 PlotLabel -> \"Figure 2.2. s(\[Lambda]t) as \[Lambda] grows\", LabelStyle -> \
figStyle,
 ImageSize -> wFull]\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[70]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c96aeb22-55c1-46a7-8e80-dfa2855dde2b"],

