Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Answer", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"65f03f38-80d5-49f5-b282-e167c777ad88"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "x sin(1/x): predicted \[Omega](\[Delta]) \[TildeTilde] 2\[Sqrt](\[Delta]/\
\[Pi]), so the error should behave like ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["N", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"-", "1"}], "/", "2"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7d5f97e2-1728-4c07-9887-a00d45d1334b"],
 ". Measured slope -0.46 over N = 5 ... 640, a little flatter than -1/2 \
because the error approaches the bound ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Omega]", "f"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3b6d4988-5b47-44c8-9823-39b100a2f1c1"],
 "(1/(2N)) from below: their ratio drifts upward, from 0.69\[Dash]0.78 at N = \
10\[Dash]40 to 0.94 at N = 640."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"e0c5fbd8-e1ed-43b4-a5d4-d3f5b36d504a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Cantor staircase: predicted \[Omega](",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["3", 
    RowBox[{"-", "k"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7d244886-eded-4e64-818d-dcfcb20a4b8c"],
 ") = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["2", 
    RowBox[{"-", "k"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c9d59871-8c4c-4a86-8f20-d4936114f48c"],
 ", so the error should behave like N to the power -ln 2/ln 3 = -0.631. \
Measured slope -0.631 for N = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["3", "k"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f1e19219-1756-4c29-ae04-cd129c1075bc"],
 ", where the error is exactly 0.931 ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Omega]", "C"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "68179a8a-f01b-4f52-a1ab-585075288d9f"],
 "(1/(2N)) at every k, and -0.62 for the other N."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"f2881d54-288b-4e86-a762-35e2f0ac7dd8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Both functions are continuous on [0, 1], so by Theorem M9 they are \
uniformly continuous, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Omega]", "f"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "82487404-0dec-4c85-8dcc-2eafea3b7efd"],
 "(1/N) \[RightArrow] 0, and the staircase converges, as Cybenko's theorem \
requires. The modulus of continuity predicts the rate: neither function is \
Lipschitz, and both converge more slowly than the O(1/N) of the smooth \
example A."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"bdaf10c0-02e8-4512-8c50-cce753bde155"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

